#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303628 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "31f3ead1-2549-5b61-baf9-0437ba28f09d" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Using the Squared Sine Variant of the Cosine Double-Angle Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d43de327-f14f-5993-b86b-37f87c7eb6bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303630} {:answer/id #uuid "9c1ea649-8300-5c98-ab16-994898b76d4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{2}-\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303631} {:answer/id #uuid "8eeeb9d9-00a8-5f46-9313-37622780ab11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303632} {:answer/id #uuid "49cc67b2-e806-56cd-a818-e2d53cbf4609" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303633} {:answer/id #uuid "c679b82c-12a6-5579-aa35-c0922a857353" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303634}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c679b82c-12a6-5579-aa35-c0922a857353" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303634} :answer-field/id #uuid "184b4d0f-01c1-54e3-9c60-6a883c5b3e9f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303629}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c4dc5f61-51e0-57b7-9717-0e7b5d819bec" :question/math-academy-id "q-114709" :question/problem "$\\int 2{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine that involves the ${sin}^{2}$ term: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta =1-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWe can rearrange the above formula as\n\n$$\n1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta =2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =\\frac{x}{4},$ we have\n\n$$\n1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})=2{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{4}).\n$$\n\nWe can now integrate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int [1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303654 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "31f3ead1-2549-5b61-baf9-0437ba28f09d" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Using the Squared Sine Variant of the Cosine Double-Angle Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e70fdbe2-e3ff-5811-8b25-f2fe769c6def" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi^3}{24}-\\frac{\\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303656}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e70fdbe2-e3ff-5811-8b25-f2fe769c6def" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi^3}{24}-\\frac{\\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303656} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb12cd62-3c0e-552b-b36a-edaad2c59d0e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303655}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3bdbba35-dcea-5023-a028-d70a659d314d" :question/math-academy-id "q-348886" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039635/assets/d1d49c23cebfe2979c8bff7b7f19d5435807a8d1aec348070aadfab1e9d2ca19.png)\n\nFind the area $A$ of the region in the $xy$-plane that is bounded by the curve $y={x}^{2}-{sin}^{2}⁡(x),$ the $x$-axis, and the lines $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $x=\\pi ,$ as shown above. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The area of the region bounded by the curve $y={x}^{2}-{sin}^{2}⁡x,$ the $x$-axis and the lines $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $x=\\pi $ is\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }({x}^{2}-{sin}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nLet's recall the double-angle formula for cosine that involves the ${sin}^{2}$ term: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x=1-2{sin}^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nWe can rearrange the above formula as\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-1)=-{sin}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nHence, the area of the region is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }({x}^{2}-{sin}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }({x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-1))\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }({x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-\\frac{1}{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x-\\frac{1}{2}x)|}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =(\\frac{{\\pi }^{3}}{3}+\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{\\pi }{2})-(\\frac{(\\pi /2)^{3}}{3}+\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{{\\pi }^{3}}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{2})-(\\frac{{\\pi }^{3}}{24}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{7{\\pi }^{3}}{24}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
